 4. 🕔 After hours: House drones 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo courtesy of Ring

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Amazon's Ring is rolling out a flying drone and roving robot, letting customers patrol the corners of their homes or businesses whether they're upstairs or out of town, Axios' Nora Eckert writes .

 Why it matters: Amazon is betting that AI can usher in a new age of autonomous security — with your camera not just watching for trouble, but actively hunting it down. 🚁 How it works: Both the Ring Drone and Rover are powered by AI Pilot, a system that uses cameras, sensors and cloud processing to navigate a home or business autonomously.

 The drone, which retails for $249.99 and will begin shipping in late January 2027, is designed to fly through corners of your home that a fixed camera can't reach. The roving security device, available for $999.99 starting in mid-December, can roam through businesses on a scheduled pattern or be sent to investigate disturbances. 💭 Nora's thought bubble: As someone who agonizes over whether I turned off the stove, this device sounds intriguing — although I expect my fiery dachshund would have different thoughts about sharing the apartment with a drone.

 
 
